WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
API signatures should communicate ownership expectations.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.200
When a function takes ownership, accepting std::unique_ptr by value makes the handoff visible.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:16.380
When a function creates an owned object for the caller, returning std::unique_ptr makes the new owner clear.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:24.720
When a function must retain shared ownership beyond the call, accepting or storing std::shared_ptr can be appropriate.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.440
Not every function that uses an object should receive a smart pointer.

6
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:37.340
If the callee only needs temporary access, a reference or raw pointer can be clearer because it says the function is borrowing, not owning.

7
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.520
A reference is useful for required non-null access.

8
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.340
A raw pointer can express optional borrowed access when null has meaning.

9
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:53.720
The important point is that borrowed access should not be stored unless the API explicitly promises that the owner outlives the stored use.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.600
Passing smart pointers everywhere can blur responsibility.

11
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.140
A function that takes shared_ptr may imply retention even when it only reads the object briefly.

12
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:11.000
A function that takes unique_ptr by reference may make reviewers wonder whether ownership is being transferred.

13
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.780
Good interfaces avoid that confusion by matching parameter type to lifetime intent.

14
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.340
Design APIs for the caller and the reviewer.

15
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.840
Name ownership-taking functions clearly.

16
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.340
Document whether access may be retained.

17
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.920
Keep nullable values explicit.

18
00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:34.500
Prefer small boundaries where raw legacy conventions are converted into RAII or smart pointer ownership.

19
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:40.920
A clear API makes misuse harder and reduces the amount of lifetime reasoning hidden in implementation details.
